# The secrets this directory's code reads. A TEMPLATE: copy it, then list yours.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and `.env.example` is not, so keep real values out of
# this file — it is the list of what to set, never what to set it to.
#
# ## One owner per env file
#
# A project usually ends up with several `.env` files, each read by different
# things and never merged. Write them down in one table, so nobody has to guess
# which file a process reads:
#
# | File                  | Read by                                    |
# | --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
# | **this one**, the root | (name the processes that read the root)    |
# | `<service>/.env`      | that service, and nothing else             |
#
# **No process reads two of them**, so there is no precedence to work out. A
# service reads only its own file, because a service that reached up to a
# checkout root would find nothing once it was installed rather than checked out.
# The process's own variables beat whichever file applies.
#
# A value needed by two files appears in both, and that is not duplication in the
# harmful sense — nothing merges them, so neither can silently shadow the other.
# Export it instead if you would rather keep one copy.
#
# ## The `secret_from` pattern
#
# Configuration that is committed (an `environments.yaml`, a settings file) names
# a secret by the VARIABLE it is read from, never by its value:
#
#     credentials:
#       reader:
#         secret_from: READER_API_KEY     # the name below, not the key itself
#
# The committed file then holds no secret, and this file is the one list of the
# names it may ask for. Change a `secret_from:` and the name here has to follow.
#
# ## Names
#
# One line per variable, blank, with a comment above saying what reads it and
# where the value comes from. No entries yet.
#
# EXAMPLE_API_KEY=
